...@_house
direction: down

source: 1. two files you write {
  class: stage

  fileA: file A declares the module {
    class: src
    label: |`prolog
      rel orchard.tree(tree_id: int, species: text)
      rel orchard.fruit(tree_id: int, picked: int)

      orchard.fruit(TreeId, Picked) <-
          orchard.tree(TreeId, _), harvest(TreeId, Picked).
    `|
  }

  fileB: file B adds rules to it {
    class: src
    label: |`prolog
      import orchard

      ripe(TreeId) <-
          orchard.fruit(TreeId, Picked), Picked > 10.
    `|
  }
}

doors: 2. the import lands one of two ways {
  class: stage

  sync: SYNC import, same compile {
    class: designed
    label: |md
      both files enter ONE compile
      names resolve at compile time
      **one DDL array, one catalog seed**
    |
    shape: rectangle
  }

  async: ASYNC import, later boot {
    class: danger
    label: |md
      file B compiles alone, arrives later
      the server boots it into the SAME db
      **two catalog seeds, ids collide**
    |
    shape: rectangle
  }
}

lowering: 3. lowering flattens the tree {
  class: stage

  mangle: every dotted name becomes one flat name {
    class: designed
    label: |`prolog
      orchard.tree/2   ->  orchard__tree__f9fc8ea9
      orchard.fruit/2  ->  orchard__fruit__3b1c07de
      ripe/1           ->  ripe
    `|
  }

  why: the digest carries path AND arity {
    class: designed
    label: |md
      `orchard.tree/2` and `orchard.tree/3`
      get DIFFERENT names, which repairs
      the shipped collision where arity is dropped
    |
    shape: rectangle
  }
}

tables: 4. what SQLite actually holds {
  class: stage

  t1: "orchard__tree__f9fc8ea9" {
    shape: sql_table
    tree_id: "INTEGER NOT NULL" {constraint: primary_key}
    species: "TEXT NOT NULL"
  }
  t2: "orchard__fruit__3b1c07de" {
    shape: sql_table
    tree_id: "INTEGER NOT NULL" {constraint: primary_key}
    picked: "INTEGER NOT NULL"
  }
  t3: ripe {
    shape: sql_table
    tree_id: "INTEGER NOT NULL" {constraint: primary_key}
  }
  note: the module itself has NO table {
    class: designed
    label: |md
      `orchard` is a name and a parent edge
      Tables exist only for rels that hold rows
    |
    shape: rectangle
  }
}

catalog: 5. the catalog, where rels point at each other {
  class: stage

  rows: __catalog_rel {
    shape: sql_table
    rel_id: "int PK" {constraint: primary_key}
    parent_id: "int, who contains me" {constraint: foreign_key}
    ordinal: "int, my argument position"
    local_name: "text, my last segment"
    kind: "primitive | rel | column"
    type_id: "int, what I am" {constraint: foreign_key}
  }

  walk: two pointers, two questions {
    class: shipped
    label: |md
      **parent_id** answers "what contains me"
      module -> rel -> column, one chain

      **type_id** answers "what am I"
      column -> primitive, or column -> another rel
    |
    shape: rectangle
  }
}

reuse: 6. why a nested rule is reusable {
  class: stage

  before: written once, inside the module {
    class: src
    label: |`prolog
      rel orchard(region) {
          fruit(TreeId, Picked) <- harvest(TreeId, Picked).
      }
    `|
  }

  after: the captured arg is pushed into every child {
    class: designed
    label: |`prolog
      orchard__fruit__3b1c07de(Region, TreeId, Picked) <-
          harvest(TreeId, Picked).
    `|
  }

  point: one body, many instantiations {
    class: designed
    label: |md
      The rule body is written ONCE.
      `Region` becomes a leading key column,
      so every instantiation shares the body
      and keeps its own rows.
    |
    shape: rectangle
  }
}

legend: what is real today {
  class: stage

  green: shipped and tested {
    class: shipped
    label: |md
      the catalog table, its parent_id
      and type_id columns, the child index
    |
    shape: rectangle
  }
  blue: designed, unbuilt {
    class: designed
    label: |md
      dotted names, imports, mangling,
      nesting, captured arguments
    |
    shape: rectangle
  }
  red: measured hazard {
    class: danger
    label: |md
      one server, one database, ids
      assigned per compile, so two
      programs overwrite each other
    |
    shape: rectangle
  }
}

source.fileA -> doors.sync: compiled together
source.fileB -> doors.sync: compiled together
source.fileB -> doors.async: compiled alone

doors.sync -> lowering.mangle: one name table
doors.async -> lowering.mangle: two name tables
lowering.mangle -> lowering.why

lowering.mangle -> tables.t1
lowering.mangle -> tables.t2
lowering.mangle -> tables.t3
lowering.mangle -> tables.note: modules skipped

tables.t1 -> catalog.rows: one rel row\nplus one row per column
tables.t2 -> catalog.rows
tables.t3 -> catalog.rows
catalog.rows -> catalog.walk

doors.async -> legend.red: this is where\nit bites

reuse.before -> reuse.after: lowering
reuse.after -> reuse.point
catalog.walk -> reuse.point: the parent edge is\nwhat makes it findable
